# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — identity and access

<!-- trailhead v{{VERSION}} -->

Who can act on this system, how they prove who they are, and what each is allowed to do.

This file exists because identity is the decision most often deferred and most expensive
to revisit. A build that shipped a single federated login as the only way in needed an
amendment and a subsystem rewrite six deliverables later, when it turned out the product
had to work for people outside one organization. Nobody made a bad call — nobody was
asked.

## The rule that catches the subtle one

> **A stable internal identity is not a login credential. Name both, separately.**

If a user's row is keyed by their email address, then changing an email is either
impossible or silently hands the account to whoever holds the new address. The same
applies to a username, a phone number, or an external provider's subject claim: they are
all *attributes that can be reassigned*, and none of them is an identity.

An identity is an opaque, permanent, internal key. Credentials — a password, a passkey, a
federated login, an API token — **link to** it, one identity to many. Getting this right
on day one costs one column. Retrofitting it costs a migration, and until that migration
lands, every provenance record you have written is attributed to something mutable.

## Principals

Every kind of actor, including the non-human ones. Machine and agent clients are where
authorization models usually leak, because they are added after the human model is
settled and inherit permissions nobody re-examined.

